          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My home blood pressure has been around 150/95 for two weeks. Is that something I need to act on now, and what do the guidelines say the target should be?",
          "answer": "Your blood pressure reading of 150/95 mm Hg over two weeks indicates a routine concern, as it falls into the stage 2 hypertension category according to guidance. It's important to note that a single reading is not enough for a diagnosis; consistent high readings require further evaluation. Therefore, it is advisable to seek medical advice. Discussing this with a clinician can help you understand your readings better and consider any necessary lifestyle or treatment changes.\n\nAccording to the guidelines, the general target for treated adults is below 130/80 mm Hg. This target may be individualized for older adults or those with certain health conditions (source: \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\"). While most authorities recommend achieving this target for most treated adults, there is some debate regarding the targets among different guidelines, especially for low-risk individuals with stage 1 hypertension (source: \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\"). \n\nAfter starting treatment, blood pressure should be monitored monthly until the target is reached; once stable, follow-up can be every three to six months (source: \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\"). If you experience symptoms such as chest pain, severe headache, or vision changes, seek immediate medical attention.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
